WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:10.080
Today, young men and women are taking different
paths into adulthood, arranging school, work

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.400
and family in a dizzying array of combinations.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:17.460
Amidst all these options there's one path
for today's young adults that's more likely

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.160
to steer them clear of poverty and towards
the American dream.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:26.880
In a new report, Wendy Wang and I find that
Millennials who took three simple steps as

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.599
part of what's called The Success Sequence
are much more likely to be doing well.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:37.870
In fact, 97% of young adults who, 1), got
at least a high school degree and, 2), held

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:44.260
down a full time job, and, 3), got married
before having any children avoided poverty

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.210
by the time they hit their early 30s.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:52.120
And, 86% of them are in the middle or upper
end of the income distribution.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.190
The Success Sequence is also associated with
markably better economic outcomes for minorities

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.700
and young adults from poor families.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.630
The bottom line: it sure looks like The Success
Sequence is a durable path towards the American

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:05.630
dream.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:11.360
To learn more about my take on the Millennial
Success Sequence, check the links in the description

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:12.360
below.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:17.610
Also, let us know what other topics you want
AEI Scholars to cover in 60 seconds.

